### Mobile Auth Phase 1 — functional test script
### NOT YET RUN. Waiting on confirmation that the configured MONGODB_URL host is
### safe to test against before this executes. Use throwaway accounts only
### (mobile-test+<timestamp>@invalid) — never real user data.
###
### Run against a local dev server: `npm run dev`, then use the VS Code REST Client
### extension (or any .http-compatible tool) against @baseUrl below.

@baseUrl = http://localhost:3000
@testEmail = mobile-test+{{$timestamp}}@invalid
@testPassword = TestPassw0rd!

### 1. Send signup verification code (existing, unchanged endpoint — reused as-is)
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/send-verification-code
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{testEmail}}"
}

### 2. Verify the code manually (read it from Mailtrap / server logs, paste below)
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/verify-signup-code
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{testEmail}}",
  "code": "REPLACE_WITH_CODE_FROM_EMAIL"
}

### 3. Register via mobile — multipart/form-data
# (send as multipart in your REST client: category=user, name, email, password,
#  accpetalltermsandcondition=true, deviceId=test-device-1, platform=ios)
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/mobile/v1/auth/register
Content-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=----boundary

------boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="category"

user
------boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="name"

Mobile Test
------boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="email"

{{testEmail}}
------boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="password"

{{testPassword}}
------boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="accpetalltermsandcondition"

true
------boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="deviceId"

test-device-1
------boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="platform"

ios
------boundary--

### Expect: { data: { accessToken, refreshToken, expiresIn, user }, error: null, meta: null }
### Save refreshToken from the response as @refreshToken1 below.

### 4. Login (should also succeed independently of register)
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/mobile/v1/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{testEmail}}",
  "password": "{{testPassword}}",
  "category": "user",
  "deviceId": "test-device-1",
  "platform": "ios"
}

### Expect: 200, { data: { accessToken, refreshToken, expiresIn, user } }
### Save this response's accessToken as @accessToken and refreshToken as @refreshToken.

@refreshToken = PASTE_FROM_STEP_4
@accessToken = PASTE_FROM_STEP_4

### 5. GET /me with bearer token
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/mobile/v1/me
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}

### Expect: 200, { data: { user } } matching the login response's user object.

### 6. Refresh
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/mobile/v1/auth/refresh
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "refreshToken": "{{refreshToken}}"
}

### Expect: 200, a NEW accessToken + refreshToken pair.
### Save the new refreshToken as @refreshTokenAfterRotation.

### 7. RACING REFRESH — call the SAME (now-old, step 4's) refreshToken again,
### twice, back-to-back, within 30 seconds of step 6. This simulates two racing
### refresh calls off the same stale token.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/mobile/v1/auth/refresh
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "refreshToken": "{{refreshToken}}"
}

###
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/mobile/v1/auth/refresh
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "refreshToken": "{{refreshToken}}"
}

### Expect: BOTH calls in step 7 succeed (each returns its own new pair — grace-window
### sibling issuance), AND the pair from step 6 (@refreshTokenAfterRotation) STILL
### works afterward (test with step 8 below). All three refresh tokens issued across
### steps 6-7 should be independently valid siblings at this point.

### 8. Confirm step 6's rotated pair is still valid (not touched by step 7's siblings)
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/mobile/v1/auth/refresh
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "refreshToken": "PASTE_refreshTokenAfterRotation_FROM_STEP_6"
}

### Expect: 200, succeeds normally.

### 9. REUSE OUTSIDE GRACE WINDOW — wait 35+ seconds, then reuse the ORIGINAL
### step-4 token (@refreshToken) again.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/mobile/v1/auth/refresh
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "refreshToken": "{{refreshToken}}"
}

### Expect: 401. AND every other token issued for this user (steps 6, 7, 8's results)
### should now also be rejected — confirm by retrying step 8's refresh call again:
### it should now 401 too (whole family revoked).

### 10. Logout
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/mobile/v1/auth/logout
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "refreshToken": "PASTE_A_CURRENTLY_VALID_REFRESH_TOKEN"
}

### Expect: 200 { data: { success: true } }. Re-running refresh with that same token
### should now 401.

### 11. Login while blocked — manually set isblocked:true on the test account in
### Mongo first (Compass or mongosh), then:
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/mobile/v1/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{testEmail}}",
  "password": "{{testPassword}}",
  "category": "user"
}

### Expect: 403 "This account has been blocked". Unset isblocked before continuing.

### 12. DELETE /me, then confirm login/refresh/social all reject afterward
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/api/mobile/v1/me
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}

### Expect: 200 { data: { success: true } }.
### Then re-run step 4 (login) with the SAME original email/password — expect
### "Invalid email or password" (since the email field was overwritten with
### deleted+<id>@invalid). Confirm in Mongo the document's email is now that
### placeholder, googleId/appleId/password are unset, and deletedAt is set.

### 13. /auth/social with a garbage token (must reject, never crash)
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/mobile/v1/auth/social
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "idToken": "not-a-real-token",
  "provider": "google"
}

### Expect: 401 "Invalid or expired social sign-in token".

### 14. Fail-closed config check — temporarily unset MOBILE_JWT_SECRET in .env,
### restart the dev server, then:
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/mobile/v1/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{testEmail}}",
  "password": "{{testPassword}}",
  "category": "user"
}

### Expect: 500 with a clear "Server misconfigured: MOBILE_JWT_SECRET is not set"
### message — never a silent/unsigned token, never a 200. Restore MOBILE_JWT_SECRET
### and restart before continuing.

### 15. Each swapped consumer route, tested TWICE — once with a real web session
### cookie (log in via the browser first, copy the next-auth.session-token cookie
### into a Cookie header here), once with a bearer token from step 4. Both must
### behave identically to how they did before this phase.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/tickets
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}

###
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/tickets
Cookie: next-auth.session-token=PASTE_WEB_SESSION_COOKIE_HERE

### Repeat the same pair of calls for: GET/POST /api/favroite, POST /api/review,
### PATCH /api/review/edit/:id, POST /api/review/delete/:id, POST /api/event/redeem,
### GET /api/user/profile, PATCH /api/user/update, and (with a real event/cart) the
### three event/ticket/{purchase,hold,hold/release} routes' guest-allowed paths (no
### Authorization header at all, confirm they still work as a true guest).

### 16. Rate-limit check — call register or guest 11+ times in under 10 minutes from
### the same IP; the 11th call should 429 "Too many registration attempts...".
